      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Who Should (and Shouldn't) Shop This Tier
        </h2>
        <p>
          We'd point a specific buyer profile toward this price range: a
          runner logging consistent weekly mileage, most weeks, not just
          occasionally, who has noticed real limitations with a mid-range
          machine, whether that's motor strain, deck flex, or cushioning
          that leaves joints sore after longer sessions. If that describes
          your situation, the durability and ride-quality gains here are
          genuinely noticeable and likely to pay off over years of use.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd also point households with multiple frequent users toward
          this tier, since the combined wear from several people using
          one machine daily accelerates the timeline on which a mid-range
          treadmill's weaker components, motor, deck, and belt, start to
          show their limits. Commercial-grade components are built
          specifically to handle that kind of cumulative, continuous use
          without the shortened service life you'd expect from a
          home-tier machine under similar demand.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the other hand, we'd steer casual walkers, occasional
          joggers, and anyone shopping primarily on a feature checklist
          rather than a genuine performance need toward a mid-range
          machine instead. The honest truth is that a well-reviewed
          $1,800 treadmill delivers a workout experience that's nearly
          indistinguishable from these premium machines for someone
          walking three or four times a week at a moderate pace. The
          differences we've described throughout this guide, frame
          rigidity under hard sprints, sustained motor output over long
          sessions, extreme incline ranges, mostly reveal themselves at
          intensities and frequencies that casual users simply don't
          reach.
        </p>
        <p>
          There's also a middle group worth mentioning: buyers who aren't
          sure yet which category they fall into, perhaps because they're
          newer to running and don't yet know if it'll become a
          consistent habit. Our advice for that group is almost always to
          start with a strong mid-range machine, build the habit over six
          months to a year, and revisit this tier once actual usage
          patterns, not aspirational ones, make the case for upgrading
          clear. Buying into this price range before establishing that
          consistency risks paying for capability that goes unused.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-black text-amber-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
