            Honest Tip
          </p>
          <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Before spending over $2,500, track your actual treadmill usage
            for a month on whatever machine you currently have, or a gym
            treadmill if you don't own one. If you're consistently using
            it 4-plus times a week and pushing into faster paces or
            incline work, this tier likely makes sense. If usage is
            sporadic, that data is worth more than any spec sheet in
            deciding whether to spend here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <h2 id="buying-considerations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Delivery, Installation, and Other Costs to Expect
        </h2>
        <p>
          Machines in this tier are substantially heavier than mid-range
          treadmills, frequently exceeding 300-350 pounds fully assembled,
          and nearly all of them arrive via freight delivery rather than a
          standard parcel carrier. Curbside freight delivery is typically
          included, but getting the machine into your home and assembled
          is where costs and logistics diverge from a standard treadmill
          purchase.
        </p>
        <p>
          White-glove delivery, which includes bringing the machine
          inside, full assembly, and packaging removal, is worth
          budgeting for at this price point, typically running an
          additional <strong>$150-$300</strong> depending on your location
          and the specific retailer. Given the weight and precision
          involved in assembling a premium treadmill correctly, we'd
          recommend it over DIY assembly for anything in this guide
          unless you have real experience and a second person to help.
        </p>
        <p>
          Floor space and ceiling clearance also deserve careful
          measurement before ordering. Several machines in this tier,
          particularly the X22i with its extreme incline range, require
          more vertical clearance than a standard treadmill to
          accommodate the frame's full range of motion at maximum incline.
          Measure your space against the manufacturer's stated clearance
          requirements, not just the machine's footprint at rest.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, factor in the flooring beneath the machine. At this
          weight and price point, a proper commercial-grade equipment mat,
          typically <strong>$80-$150</strong> for a size appropriate to
          these larger frames, both protects your floor from the
          machine's weight over time and meaningfully reduces vibration
          transfer, particularly relevant if you're on an upper floor or
          share walls with neighbors.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
