        </h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is a treadmill over $2,500 actually worth it for most home users?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For most casual walkers and occasional joggers, no. The jump
              in this tier mainly benefits frequent runners logging
              significant weekly mileage, buyers who specifically want
              extreme incline training, or households where multiple
              people put heavy, sustained wear on the machine. If you run
              under 10-15 miles a week and don't need incline training
              beyond a standard range, a $1,500-$2,000 machine likely
              covers your needs just as well.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What specifically justifies spending over $2,500 on a treadmill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Three things consistently justified the price in our
              testing: motor and frame durability rated for genuinely
              heavy, sustained use rather than occasional sessions;
              incline ranges that go meaningfully beyond a standard
              treadmill, like the X22i's <strong>40% maximum grade</strong>;
              and ride quality refinements, like Life Fitness's FlexDeck
              system, that are noticeable specifically to frequent,
              higher-mileage runners rather than casual users.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do premium treadmills still require an iFit or similar subscription?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It depends on the brand. NordicTrack's premium machines
              remain iFit-dependent for most of their standout features
              regardless of price. Life Fitness and Matrix Fitness
              machines in this tier generally do not require a
              subscription for core functionality, reflecting their
              commercial-equipment heritage where subscription models are
              less standard.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              When is spending this much overkill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              If your workouts are primarily walking, if you use the
              treadmill a few times a week rather than daily, or if you
              don't have a strong preference around incline training or
              ride refinement, you're very likely to be just as satisfied
              with a well-reviewed machine in the $1,500-$2,500 range.
              Spending more in that scenario buys durability headroom you
              won't need and features you won't use.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How much more durable are these machines really?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Meaningfully more, particularly for machines like the Matrix
              T7xe and Life Fitness Platinum Club Series that use
              genuinely commercial-rated components rather than home-tier
              parts. These machines are engineered and tested for
              continuous multi-hour daily use in gym settings, which
              translates to a substantial durability buffer for home use,
              even heavy home use, well beyond what standard home
              treadmills are built to withstand.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Should I buy new or consider a certified refurbished commercial unit at this price?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It's worth researching both. Certified refurbished
              commercial treadmills from reputable dealers can sometimes
              deliver similar hardware to what's covered in this guide at
              a lower price, though warranty coverage and condition vary
              significantly by seller, so we'd only recommend that route
              through an established, reputable refurbisher rather than
              an unverified private sale.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

